WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.883 --> 00:00:09.300
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: I'm a product
manager in cloud networking.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.100
I work on a bunch of
different technologies,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.895
including related to
cloud load balancing CDN

00:00:14.895 --> 00:00:17.930
and, more recently, on-service
mesh and some of the newer

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.170
paradigms.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.620
And one of the reasons we put
this panel together is there

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.350
are several talks where
we talk about products.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.880
So we talk about solutions.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.590
And everywhere,
the theme is open.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.780
And it's hybrid, and it's
enabling new businesses.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.907
Nowhere have we really
paused to really say, OK,

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:35.740
what does open mean?

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.590
Especially, it might
mean different things

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:38.960
to a different set of people.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.440
And then what are
the commonalities

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.830
amongst all of those people?

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:43.510
What are they trying to achieve?

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.110
And so I thought I'd get four
very unique perspectives.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.510
I'll let these folks
introduce themselves.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.940
But let's see what
open means to them,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.810
and then we will also have a Q&amp;A
for 15 minutes in the middle,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.820
where you get a chance
to ask them questions.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.460
So I'll call that out once
we get to that section.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.910
But with that, I'd like to start
with our first panelist, Anna.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.310
And introduce yourself.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.230
ANNA BERENBERG: Hi,
I'm Anna Berenberg,

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.250
and I'm a tech lead
for load balancing,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.970
and one of the product today
was announced, Traffic Director.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.610
I've been at Google
13 years, and Prajakta

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:22.890
is my partner in crime.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.788
We work together on
a lot of products.

00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:28.580
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Hi,
I'm Kelsey Hightower.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.440
I'm kind of a generalist these
days at Google, focused mainly

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.890
on containers and
now serverless stuff

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.510
and have to deal
with these folks

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.150
to get the networking
the way I want it.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.527
And they're great, too.

00:01:40.527 --> 00:01:41.610
MATT KLEIN: Hey, everyone.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.750
My name is Matt Klein.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.500
I'm a software
engineer at Lyft, where

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.980
I work mostly on
infrastructure and networking.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.160
I spend a lot of time these
days working on a network

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.270
proxy called Envoy.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.490
BIKASH KOLEY: Bikash Koley, I'm
the CTO of Juniper Networks.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.770
Before that, I spent
almost 10 years

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:02.870
at Google building
the production network

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:05.343
that a lot of the
Google services run on.

00:02:05.343 --> 00:02:06.760
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
I'll probably just

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.090
add some more, because
they've been extremely modest.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.460
So Anna drives a lot of cloud
networking from the inside.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.800
She's one of the seven
distinguished engineer

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.540
females at Google.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.230
Kelsey is the face of Kubernetes
and Cloud Run and now Istio

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.420
and a whole bunch of things.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:25.420
Matt is the one who
developed Envoy at Lyft.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.920
And Bikash is the
one who developed

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.690
all of Google's
infrastructure and now is

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:29.860
doing the same at Juniper.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.340
So I'm really excited
to have them here.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.740
I wanted to start
the question-- and we

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.140
can keep it free
flowing, but I wanted

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.450
to start off this panel
with a question, which

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.160
is, we talk so often about open,
and we talk about open systems.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.690
Does it really mean systems?

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.010
Does it mean APIs?

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.630
Does it mean something else?

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.410
I wanted to get your perspective
on what open means for you.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.510
And maybe we start from
that end this time.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.463
So maybe, Bikash, we
can start with you.

00:02:57.463 --> 00:02:58.190
BIKASH KOLEY: OK.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:58.870
Thank you.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.020
So open, from my
perspective, I see

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.920
three layers of open as it
starts with open standards,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.250
goes to open API, then
it goes to open source.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:14.320
And from our perspective,
it's ultimately something

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.110
that the customers
increasingly want, or need,

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:21.490
or demand from the
perspective of how do I

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:22.660
get innovation velocity?

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.910
How do I have control
on what I'm actually

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.180
deploying on my
infrastructure without having

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.780
the supplier of the
technology having to do that?

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.020
And how do I ensure security?

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.090
How do I get the maximum
number of eyes on the product

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.180
that we're building?

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.530
So open cuts through that
layer, but increasingly, it's

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:42.220
a business need.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.890
That's how I see it.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.180
MATT KLEIN: Yeah, that
was a fantastic answer.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.360
Probably would have been
pretty close to my own.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.070
I think the thing that I would
add is, open, to me, it also--

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.990
via open APIs, open source,
all of those things,

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.560
it allows us to build
more powerful platforms.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.180
So one of the
things that I think

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.380
we've been able to
do with Envoy is,

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.890
because we have so many
companies and people adopting

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.530
it, we're seeing a
cycle of essentially

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.330
vertical applications
that are starting

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.000
to form around that technology.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.040
And I think whereas
historically we

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.420
have competing proprietary
technologies that

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.560
tend to focus their efforts
on a very singular fashion.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.500
Now, yes, we can compete
on vertical axes.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.160
But it allows us, I think, to
actually make more progress.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.660
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
I think, for me, I

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.030
used to say it was kind
of rooted in open source.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.060
These days, I care
a little bit less

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.160
about that as long
as I can solve

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.080
my own problems without waiting
for the vendor to do it for me.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.480
If I can get that
in proprietary,

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.120
or open, or open
source project, I

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.570
feel that gives me the power
to solve my own problems.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:55.810
So I think that's
the key tenet for me

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.810
because there's some open
source projects, where

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.330
you can't actually extend it
to solve your own problems.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.520
So that doesn't necessarily
fit the bill for me.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:09.230
ANNA BERENBERG: Three for
me-- it's operatability,

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:13.370
interoperability,
and open standards.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:18.950
All of them are because we want
our customers to write code

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:20.660
once and run it everywhere.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.920
As you heard probably yesterday
and today about Anthos.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:26.990
It's the same thing, right?

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.140
Basically, what we want,
we want to build systems,

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.400
that whole underneath
horizontals where you can--

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.240
on which you can build your
business logic, one workload.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.170
And because of portability
on underlying systems,

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:45.890
you can run it on-prem
and on multiple clouds.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:48.140
Also, about
interoperability, that

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.470
means that control planes need
to talk to each other, right?

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:54.080
Because just running
it on each cloud

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.930
separately doesn't
help you to make it

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:00.920
into a single deployment,
which you also want to have.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:01.910
And how you can do it?

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:05.030
You can do it only
with open standards,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.950
because nobody wants
a vendor lock-in.

00:06:08.698 --> 00:06:10.615
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Actually,
that is interesting.

00:06:10.615 --> 00:06:14.350
And when I take your
definitions of open,

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:17.680
and then I think back to a lot
of the open technologies each

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.377
of you has been
involved in, I think,

00:06:19.377 --> 00:06:21.460
what would be really
fascinating for the audience,

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.430
because it's
fascinating for me, is--

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.630
so just as an
example, Matt, you've

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.115
been involved with Envoy.

00:06:28.115 --> 00:06:29.740
I used to always
wonder, why didn't you

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.930
not make a startup out of it?

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.530
You could have had this
really fancy startup.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.432
So in my mind, the
question-- and I'm

00:06:37.432 --> 00:06:38.890
sure a lot of folks
in the audience

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:42.700
as well-- is, these
open source products

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.320
that you've been involved,
what was the starting point

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:45.820
for them?

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:46.930
How did you think of them?

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.907
How did they end up
landing in open source?

00:06:49.907 --> 00:06:51.490
What was your journey,
and what do you

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:53.500
think you would have done
differently, in retrospect,

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:54.310
for those projects?

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:56.802
And maybe we can
start with you, Matt.

00:06:56.802 --> 00:06:57.760
MATT KLEIN: Sure, yeah.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.740
So I think, one of the things
that has made Envoy so popular

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.250
is that it wasn't
started as a product

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:07.600
that we were going to sell.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.500
We developed it at Lyft to solve
particular customer problems.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.190
I've worked in the networking
space for quite some years,

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.650
and I had a feeling that
maybe this thing would

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.780
be useful to folks outside.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:23.500
I did not understand what would
happen once we open sourced.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:26.410
It's been a really
incredible journey.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:31.720
But that question of starting
a company and being open,

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:33.580
it's one that's
top of mind to me,

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.250
and I could talk for a
very long time about this,

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:37.780
so I'll stop myself.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.330
But I think the key
is, a lot of people

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.820
right now are trying to
understand how to make money

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.330
around open source.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.330
It's a very complicated topic.

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:50.260
And I think there's two
things that are often at odds,

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.260
and those are about
having open technologies,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.880
building a larger community.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.740
And very often,
when you're trying

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:01.270
to make money on something
in a singular fashion,

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:02.950
you might be looking
at business models,

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.380
like open core, where
you're blocking features.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:08.450
And that tends to antagonize
the larger community.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:10.750
So when people ask
me about, hey, Matt,

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.720
why didn't you go
and start a company.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:15.970
We can talk after,
but I don't think

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:19.210
Envoy would be what it is
today if I had left and started

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:19.750
a company.

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.580
We would not have
all three major cloud

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:22.510
providers using Envoy.

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:25.240
We wouldn't have tons of
companies building products

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:28.240
on top of Envoy
because they would all

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.200
be competing with
me and my company,

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:34.682
and that does not make a
larger and vibrant community.

00:08:34.682 --> 00:08:35.890
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: And I think--

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.929
I mean, that's a really
interesting perspective.

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:40.510
And I wanted to add in
Bikash's perspective.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:43.240
He comes from a
vendor, and then you've

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.430
had your journey
with OpenContrail,

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:47.100
which is now Tungsten Fabric.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.690
And it's a little bit different
from what Matt described.

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:53.100
So can you take us through that?

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.400
What is Juniper?

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.030
What were you thinking as you
took it through that journey?

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.410
BIKASH KOLEY: Yeah,
so Tungsten Fabric,

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.950
which originated in
Contrail five-plus years

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:08.650
back, the reason that it was
open sourced, as OpenContrail,

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.560
in those days, was, because
the customers actually

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.100
asked Juniper to do it.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.850
And the reason is sort
of what I described

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:18.910
before, that, ultimately,
especially in this space,

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:20.170
companies come, companies go.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.210
When somebody is investing their
core infrastructure examples

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.190
would be very large carriers.

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:29.680
All of their calls go
over this infrastructure.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.260
When you are betting that
mission-critical application

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:33.973
on something, you
want to, ultimately,

00:09:33.973 --> 00:09:35.890
control your own destiny
one way or the other.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:40.810
And having a code
base that's open

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:43.570
allows you to, ultimately,
control your own destiny,

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.912
if you have to, at that point.

00:09:45.912 --> 00:09:46.870
It has come a long way.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:50.530
And really, what
our vision is now

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.320
with Tungsten Fabric, the
reason that it changed

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:55.450
from OpenContrail
to Tungsten Fabric,

00:09:55.450 --> 00:10:00.840
is I believe that the new cloud
stack is built on open source.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.500
It starts from Linux
to KVM to Kubernetes.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:09.240
Even before that, OpenStack,
OpenShift, or [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.450
and so on and so forth.

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:13.180
It's built on open source.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:16.930
This stack needs
a networking stack

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.360
that doesn't make assumption
as to where your workload is,

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.602
or which cloud it's running
on, or where it's deployed,

00:10:22.602 --> 00:10:24.310
or whether it's
greenfield or brownfield.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.000
I mean, as much as all of us
would love all the applications

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.850
to be containerized
and service mesh best,

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.100
a large part of the world
still runs their application

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:33.700
on bare metal.

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:38.110
Now how do you get that
deployment on the same network

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.450
stack that allows
you to actually move

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:43.000
and migrate very quickly to
the modern ways of writing

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.730
application, but you
don't leave things behind?

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:47.698
And I saw that
stack was missing.

00:10:47.698 --> 00:10:49.240
There were a lot of
networking stacks

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:51.880
that were designed for
very specific things.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:53.380
But there isn't an
open source stack

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.547
that actually allows you
to do all of this together.

00:10:55.547 --> 00:10:58.580
And frankly, if you are
trying to go into multicloud,

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.600
and if you're serious
about it, multicloud

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:01.963
are the largest public clouds.

00:11:01.963 --> 00:11:03.130
But they're also open style.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:03.970
They're also VMware.

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.940
They're also bare metal
cloud that they have built.

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:08.620
So we saw the need,
and we saw the gap.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:12.520
And it was it was sort of
the obvious thing for us

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:15.620
to sort of expand the
open source charter

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.680
that OpenContrail had
into Tungsten Fabric.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:21.770
And it has been a
fabulous journey.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.600
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Awesome.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:27.577
Now, I'm taking you back to some
of the early days of Google.

00:11:27.577 --> 00:11:30.160
And I know, Kelsey, you've been
heavily involved in Kubernetes

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:31.878
for a really, really long time.

00:11:31.878 --> 00:11:33.670
In my mind, you are
the face of Kubernetes,

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.920
and then now, this Cloud Run
and a bunch of new things

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.270
you're playing around with.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:39.580
When you think of
Kubernetes, I hear

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.950
you speak very
passionately on the topic.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.270
What is that journey?

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:46.150
And then what were the tenets
in mind, where you said,

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:48.920
oh, it's going to solve this
set of problems really well?

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:51.170
And I also know there are
certain cases where you say,

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:52.510
do not use Kubernetes there.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:56.030
So I actually want to hear
of it from the beginning.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:57.580
So what got you into Kubernetes?

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:00.010
What was the fascinating
aspect of it, the community,

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:01.803
and so on?

00:12:01.803 --> 00:12:03.970
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So I
think, early with Kubernetes,

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:07.200
it's Google's entry
into the container place

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.368
where they have the most
background and expertise

00:12:10.368 --> 00:12:11.910
at that particular
checkpoint, right?

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:13.420
So Docker is dominant.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:14.230
I'm at CoreOS.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.800
There's a startup trying to
build Google's infrastructure

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:17.970
for everyone else.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:21.190
And then Google comes along
and does exactly that.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.360
So if you recognize that,
if that was your mission,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.210
and it's now playing out--
and Google was very inviting.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:30.000
I was able to contribute,
get things merged in.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.845
And at that
particular time, there

00:12:31.845 --> 00:12:35.010
was this kind of friendly
war, the container wars.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.620
Lots of us have scars from
the container war days.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:40.080
And we were all trying to
capitalize on this ability

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.230
to package your app and run
it in a very consistent way.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.970
But that wasn't
necessarily solved

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:46.567
across multiple machines.

00:12:46.567 --> 00:12:48.150
And Kubernetes added
a lot of features

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.850
to make that as easy
as possible, ideally,

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.280
competing with things
like Docker Swarm,

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.415
Nomad, all of these
kind of easy-to-use,

00:12:55.415 --> 00:12:56.790
distributed
computing things that

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:59.190
just try to make your
environment look like something

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:00.365
like App Engine.

00:13:00.365 --> 00:13:01.740
There was one
pivotal point where

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.490
we understood that the
power of Kubernetes

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:05.060
was not going to just be that.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.990
I remember, we were doing some
empathy sessions internally.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:10.920
And one thing that was very
clear is that Kubernetes API

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.282
and when we added CRDs,
Custom Resource Definitions,

00:13:14.282 --> 00:13:15.990
to me, that was the
biggest game-changer.

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:18.510
Kubernetes is not just
a container runtime.

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.300
That's the first system that
was built on top of Kubernetes.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.717
So there was a
phrase that I said.

00:13:22.717 --> 00:13:25.200
It's Kubernetes is as a
platform for building platforms.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.362
The thing about having
APIs and extensions,

00:13:27.362 --> 00:13:28.570
of course, you can extend it.

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:30.750
But you can only do what
the interface provides.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:32.340
Hey, here's a network extension.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.430
You can only do with
the interface provides.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:37.020
In the Kubernetes world was
we made it a very first-class

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.970
thing to make custom
extensions that

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:41.130
had nothing to do with
the rest of the platform.

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:42.900
You want to build
a CI/CD system?

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.600
Make a CRD, and we would
generate all the client

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:46.530
bindings.

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:49.540
Our back will be applied,
even to your custom extension.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:52.620
So we became a framework
for building extensions.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.470
And that's very different than
just having a plugin interface

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:56.970
over a limited framework.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.743
And that was key,
to me, of letting

00:13:58.743 --> 00:13:59.910
people solve their problems.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:03.070
So to me, it wasn't just
because Kubernetes was open.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.190
It was because it was this
open framework for you

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:08.430
to solve any problem that
you wanted to, regardless

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:10.577
if you got community
consensus or not.

00:14:10.577 --> 00:14:12.660
I think that was the pivotal
point when Kubernetes

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.740
took a life of its own.

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:18.110
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: And
coming to Anna, the reason

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.770
I saved Anna for the last is
I have a lot of visibility

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:25.910
into some of the systems
she's built at Google.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:27.110
Oftentimes.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.510
we hear that, oh, but
we are not Google.

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:31.623
And I think, a lot
of the paradigms that

00:14:31.623 --> 00:14:33.290
were built at Google
are not necessarily

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:34.820
meant only for Google.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.390
And a lot of the
systems were born,

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:39.110
where we wanted to
abstract away the toil,

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.270
but then still give you
all of the benefits, so you

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:43.800
can have the same benefits
for your services.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:45.230
And so I wanted
Anna to just talk

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:48.620
a little bit about the systems
you built, for instance,

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:49.580
12 years back.

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.863
And now, you're building
systems out for cloud.

00:14:51.863 --> 00:14:53.030
How do you see that journey?

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:54.590
What kind of systems are they?

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.420
How different are
they today from what

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:59.410
you built 12 years back?

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:05.410
ANNA BERENBERG: Well,
I started in 2006.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:09.300
And at the time, Google had
couple data centers, maybe

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.760
three, four at the time,
maybe a little more.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:17.560
But we had a load balancer,
and the load balancer

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:20.500
that was in place was a
[? square ?] load balancer,

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.120
and it was falling in pieces.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:28.270
So we build totally new control
plane load balancer global

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:30.190
that done wonderful work.

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:34.690
And we organically grew it
based on the experiences

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.910
with these big
customers that we had,

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:41.320
which was Web Search,
YouTube, Gmail, each of which

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.920
had a very different
requirements.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:47.470
But most of them turned
into requirements

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:50.390
that are pretty much
common to everybody else.

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:56.030
They want to send data
as fast as possible.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:58.720
They want to minimize
the round-trip time

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:01.900
for overall traffic globally.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.920
They want to make sure they
can run their workload as

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.020
cheap as possible.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.650
So that means that they want
load balancer to distribute

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:12.490
traffic in a way that it will
optimize overall capacity.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:15.250
And they want to make sure
they can control and debug

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:18.280
things, so their
administrative tools that

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:21.840
built into the traffic
management and stuff.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:24.670
And we thought, this
is so Google, right?

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:26.950
And then we go into
Cloud, and guess what.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:28.900
Our customers wanted the same.

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:33.070
The interesting thing, though,
that we learned a lot about it

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.480
over years.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:36.490
And the customers are
pretty sophisticated,

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:39.640
and they want just
right this, this moment,

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:42.980
exactly this that we built
over such a long time.

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:46.650
So it's not like Google
is very different.

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:50.050
We were a little
isolated and lived

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.860
in this self kind of bubble.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:57.880
And the customers'
needs growing very fast,

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:01.000
and their requirements
are outpacing

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.790
at this point internal
Google requirements, which

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:06.352
is interesting.

00:17:06.352 --> 00:17:07.560
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Sounds great.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:10.208
And then just building
on what Bikash said,

00:17:10.208 --> 00:17:11.750
one of the interesting
things he said

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:15.230
is, as much as we want
all of these technologies

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:18.359
to appear in our networks and
our deployments, the world--

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:20.630
I mean, there is a lot
of existing deployments.

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:23.060
And we need to figure out
how to get these deployments

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.099
to whatever is the
end state based

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:26.750
on the goals of the customers.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:28.667
Now one of the things
I hear about all

00:17:28.667 --> 00:17:30.750
of these technologies--
and I actually believe it,

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.890
but I also hear
about it-- is they

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.970
are providing a lot of ways to
solve the challenges that we

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:39.500
face in a multicloud world.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:41.610
And even when we look
at cloud customers,

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.180
and I'm sure, as you look
at your own requirements,

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:46.070
you probably have a
deployment on-prem.

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:48.140
And then you are
in Google Cloud,

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.280
and you're likely in
other clouds as well.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.940
And then it's extremely hard
to deploy and manage these.

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.780
So one of the questions-- and I
want to see what their opinion

00:17:56.780 --> 00:17:57.440
is--

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.650
do you believe fundamentally
that these systems

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.290
can be game-changers
for enabling you

00:18:03.290 --> 00:18:06.830
to deploy services,
manage them seamlessly?

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:08.630
Is there a big gap
that needs to be

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:10.430
filled before we can say that?

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:11.730
What is your take on that?

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.692
And any of you can start.

00:18:13.692 --> 00:18:15.650
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I think
the biggest problem--

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:17.120
we've seen this--

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:19.730
every time there's new
technology that comes out,

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:23.390
there are some people who buy
what the vendor is selling.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:24.560
They read the blog post.

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.400
They copy and
paste and retrofit,

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.468
not really understanding
the fundamentals.

00:18:29.468 --> 00:18:31.510
There are people who
understand the fundamentals,

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.427
and look at these new
tools, and evaluate them

00:18:33.427 --> 00:18:35.350
on their ability
to solve problems

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:37.043
in that fundamental space.

00:18:37.043 --> 00:18:39.460
I think the challenge is, we
have so many tools coming out

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:41.483
now, the pace of those tools.

00:18:41.483 --> 00:18:43.150
And you get really
confused if you don't

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:44.710
know what the fundamentals are.

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:48.400
At the fundamental, you can look
at Nginx, HAProxy, and Envoy

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:50.657
as doing very basically
the same thing.

00:18:50.657 --> 00:18:52.240
And there's a higher
level fundamental

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:53.593
around configuration.

00:18:53.593 --> 00:18:54.760
How do you configure things?

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.093
Most people are familiar with
configuring these via file

00:18:57.093 --> 00:18:58.163
and restarting something.

00:18:58.163 --> 00:18:59.830
And you look at Envoy,
and you say, wow,

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.350
this XTS protocol to
incrementally configure

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.547
a thing live, that's a big
transition for most people.

00:19:05.547 --> 00:19:07.630
So that's how I fundamentally
look at these things

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:09.940
based on fundamentals
and say, wow, Envoy

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.400
is really bringing
something new to the table.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.320
Fundamentally, it's
routing packets around.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.600
But there's something
different in the way you think.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.725
In order to take advantage
of something like Envoy,

00:19:18.725 --> 00:19:20.650
you have to
understand, the ability

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:23.440
to configure something that sits
in the network the way it is

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:27.190
sets the stage for things like
Istio and Traffic Director.

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:28.930
Without that
understanding, all of this

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:30.940
just looks like
noise and confusion,

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:32.773
and then someone has
to try to convince you.

00:19:32.773 --> 00:19:34.148
And I think,
unfortunately, we've

00:19:34.148 --> 00:19:36.250
been trying to do that to
the sales motion and not

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:37.930
the engineering
emotion of saying,

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:39.910
this is what these
things actually are.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:40.942
Here's what they do.

00:19:40.942 --> 00:19:43.150
And it's OK if you want to
outsource that to someone.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:45.483
But you got to understand
what that technology is first,

00:19:45.483 --> 00:19:48.480
so you can take advantage of it.

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:50.850
MATT KLEIN: Yeah, I
think the biggest problem

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:56.130
that I see is, like Kelsey said,
there's so many technologies

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:57.120
coming out right now.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:59.790
And there's this tension that
we have from the tools, where--

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:01.740
and I see this all
the time with Envoy--

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.530
where we have a section of
people that come to Envoy.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:07.210
And they'll say, I
don't understand this.

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:08.530
It's too complicated.

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:10.300
There's too many
configuration options.

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:12.100
I just don't even
know where to start.

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:14.670
And then we have another set
of people that come and build

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:18.120
absolutely incredible
things that blow my mind

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:21.780
without ever talking to me or
anyone else on the project.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:23.400
So we have this tension.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:25.800
Clearly, we have
many different users,

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:28.560
from people that want a
prepackaged solution to people

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:32.160
that want an extensible solution
to build vertical things.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:32.820
And

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:37.080
I think the key takeaway for
me is that there is never

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:43.780
going to be a one thing that
fits every possible end case.

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:46.350
And I think that, from
the infrastructure side,

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:48.540
we have to build
layered solutions.

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:49.950
So there's going
to be something,

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:53.010
like Kubernetes, or like Envoy,
which is more of a platform.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:55.540
And then we might have vertical
solutions on top of that.

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:58.530
And then we might have pass
or functional platforms that

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:00.540
sit on top and or
more opinionated,

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:03.270
as we actually get
further up the stack.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.330
And I think, right
now, we're still

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:09.960
in that very early days of
creating a lot of tools,

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:13.750
and there's a lot of
confused people out there.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:15.900
And I think we have
to iterate to find

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.610
the right layers
of abstraction, so

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:19.800
that people can tap
in at the layer that's

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:20.940
most appropriate for them.

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.700
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I
want to add one thing.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:26.730
And I see this a
lot with technology.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:28.470
When new technology
comes out, there's

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:30.770
a large population
in the tech world

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:33.590
that wants the new thing
to work the old way.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:36.210
And this is what really slows
down some of these projects,

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:37.207
some of the momentum.

00:21:37.207 --> 00:21:39.290
You start adding a lot of
cruft to these projects,

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:41.100
that they shouldn't
be doing these things.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.400
But in an attempt to
grow your user base,

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:46.310
sometimes you will try to
retrofit this new thing that

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.230
has bought into
this new paradigm

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:49.340
to solve the old problems.

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.890
And sometimes, you just got
to draw the line and say, no.

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.470
That's not what this thing does,
even if it does cost market

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:58.430
share or different use cases.

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:00.910
BIKASH KOLEY: So I am
a technologist just

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:03.680
like everybody
else in the panel.

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:06.040
I'll share a slightly
different perspective.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:10.170
I spend a lot of time in
my role with customers.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:16.420
And I think, as technologists,
we often forget usability.

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:21.610
We go after look how cool this
thing is with what it does,

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:27.140
but not how it actually
solves a customer's problem.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:29.350
You sort of touched upon
that in the abstraction,

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:31.930
but it's actually
more than abstraction.

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:36.590
If we don't understand
the current state

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:39.070
of an infrastructure
of a customer,

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:42.460
and if we show them the
shining city on the hill,

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:44.710
and we don't show them the
steps to get to the shining

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.570
city on the hill,
and we tell them,

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:49.180
by the way, where you live
today, condemned house,

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:50.120
forget it.

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:50.930
It's not reality.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:52.840
It's not reality
for any one of us.

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:54.760
I have yet to see--

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:56.890
and I can only
speak from network.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.230
I've built infrastructure
for a long time at Google.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:02.170
I speak to a lot of
customers all the time.

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:04.380
I've yet to see a single
instance of a greenfield

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:05.005
infrastructure.

00:23:05.005 --> 00:23:06.270
It doesn't exist.

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.350
It's fantasy.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:11.500
So if it is fantasy,
then the reality is,

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:12.730
we've got to build the steps.

00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:14.440
If we don't build the
steps in technology,

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:16.898
it doesn't matter how cool the
shining city on the hill is.

00:23:16.898 --> 00:23:19.240
It's never going to be real.

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:25.870
My view is that the
technology world has often

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:28.280
ignored the steps.

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:30.280
And that does not
mean that you actually

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:33.550
drag your old broken house
up to the shining city.

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:36.730
But it means that you give a
way to get to there instead,

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:39.315
where you are not layering
yet another set of tools, yet

00:23:39.315 --> 00:23:41.050
another set of infrastructure.

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:43.210
Unfortunately,
one of the reasons

00:23:43.210 --> 00:23:45.670
for the explosion
of the tools is

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:49.280
because we haven't quite solved
the old, and we have a new one.

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:52.330
And so we have layered
yet another tool, right?

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:54.970
If I happen to
have infrastructure

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:58.510
that must run on my
15-year-old database,

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:00.520
because that's where
I have 100 million

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:02.620
of my customers'
information, no way I

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:05.520
can migrate to something
else in the next five years.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.130
Well, I still have to run that.

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:09.588
And if I tell them that you're
going to run that in the way

00:24:09.588 --> 00:24:12.310
that we ran 15 years back,
which was throwing people at it,

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:14.800
but you have all these new
shiny applications that

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.580
is fully-automated,
intent-driven, policy-operated,

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:22.270
and this is a new way of
training your workforce

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.550
and operators, I don't
think that's real.

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:27.880
Because I don't think anybody
has money to actually do it.

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:30.520
One of the beauty of public
cloud is that underneath,

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:32.110
the infrastructure
always changes.

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:34.450
The stable APIs hide that.

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:36.972
I know for a fact that GCP
seven years back is not

00:24:36.972 --> 00:24:38.680
what GCP is today with
the infrastructure

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:39.980
that it has underneath.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:44.620
But if somebody used APIs
on GCP, most of those APIs

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:45.970
are still the same.

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:47.830
I think, when the
tools start doing that,

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:51.460
where it's not really
forgetting the migration, that's

00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:53.120
an important part
for us to solve.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:56.230
We are definitely focusing a lot
on that with Tungsten Fabric,

00:24:56.230 --> 00:24:58.340
because most of the
customers that we see,

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.580
they absolutely want the
worldwide service mesh

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:03.450
where security is not
a networking thing.

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:05.200
Security is an
application thing.

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:08.140
But they also run most of their
applications on bare metal,

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:10.460
maybe on VM in some scenarios.

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:12.880
And if I tell them that
your policy construct

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:16.098
for our service mesh is
this, but in the old way,

00:25:16.098 --> 00:25:17.890
you're still configuring
firewall policies,

00:25:17.890 --> 00:25:20.830
OK, we haven't really
solved the problem for them.

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:23.770
So I actually do
believe that bridging

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:25.510
the gap between
the old and the new

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:28.000
is a critically important
component of the technology.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:29.800
And those that will
do it, they'll win.

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:32.386
Because the customers need that.

00:25:32.386 --> 00:25:34.750
ANNA BERENBERG: Some
time ago, I set up

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:38.230
to build intent-driven
configuration

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:41.320
system for service management.

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:44.080
It turned out into
success, but it only

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:51.295
worked-- for 50% of services
worked well, for 35%,

00:25:51.295 --> 00:25:55.090
it worked somewhat, and
it didn't work for 15%.

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:58.600
And those 15% were probably
the most important one.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:00.110
They're big.

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:05.680
They need every little
knob you can even think of.

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:10.890
And with that, I understood
that we need everything.

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:13.990
We need the knobs
as the foundation,

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:16.120
and we need to
have them exposed.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:23.140
We need a simpler configuration
on the top to help the 35%,

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:24.910
and we do need intent.

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:31.810
So I think we're at a
point in the cloud era

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:34.010
where we're still
at the knob level.

00:26:34.010 --> 00:26:37.300
And I think if we
do the knobs well,

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:40.810
then the verticals on the top of
it and the intent-based system

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:43.020
will grow next.

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:44.770
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I
want to add one thing.

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:48.030
So before coming to Google, I've
been in your position, trying

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:49.490
to manage infrastructure.

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:52.020
Maybe you share
the same opinion.

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:55.420
From a provider, we have
to try to support everyone.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:57.670
When you're at your own
company, you only care kind of

00:26:57.670 --> 00:26:59.110
about your use case, right?

00:26:59.110 --> 00:27:01.735
I've been in cases, where we
burnt the whole house down,

00:27:01.735 --> 00:27:02.860
so we can have the new one.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:03.820
And it's painful.

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:04.930
Not everyone can do it.

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:06.867
But you can do it.

00:27:06.867 --> 00:27:08.450
But as providers,
we are kind of stuck

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:10.367
in this situation where
we're trying to go out

00:27:10.367 --> 00:27:11.430
of a large market share.

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:12.980
So we kind of have
these tug of wars

00:27:12.980 --> 00:27:14.915
internally on what the
feature set should be.

00:27:14.915 --> 00:27:16.790
And I think there's just
different people who

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:18.770
are concerned with
different parts of that.

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:21.590
When I looked at Envoy, I
looked at it as a clean start.

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:24.867
There's a clean start here to
solve a very unique use case.

00:27:24.867 --> 00:27:27.200
I think Docker came from the
same thing as a clean start

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.120
to solve a unique use case.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.160
And then once it gets
mass adoption, I think,

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:33.080
you start to have the
ecosystems show up.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:35.600
And there are people who will
help you adopt that thing,

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.220
build a bridge technology.

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:40.070
But I hope that we are free to
continue to build things that

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:41.480
don't work in the old world.

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:43.520
And then maybe someone
will come and bridge it.

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:44.930
But I don't want us
to try to figure out

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:45.972
how to bridge everything.

00:27:45.972 --> 00:27:48.980
Because I think it will stifle
new ways of doing things,

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:51.050
because we're always
constantly stuck

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:52.550
with the 20-year-old decisions.

00:27:55.418 --> 00:27:57.710
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So I'm going
to open it up to audience.

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:00.020
Before that, I pulled
out a bunch of questions

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:03.263
from the Dory, and then also
from LinkedIn and Twitter.

00:28:03.263 --> 00:28:05.180
And there's some really
interesting questions.

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:06.638
There was one for
you, Kelsey, that

00:28:06.638 --> 00:28:10.070
said, how do you become
good at so many open source

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.090
technologies?

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:14.390
These people are having probably
some fan club out there.

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:17.173
But they're having hard
time just getting their arms

00:28:17.173 --> 00:28:18.590
around Kubernetes,
and then you're

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:21.030
already onto Cloud Run and
a bunch of different things.

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:21.947
So how do you do that?

00:28:21.947 --> 00:28:24.405
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So 10 years
ago, I came to the conclusion

00:28:24.405 --> 00:28:26.200
that there is probably
nothing new, period.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:26.700
It isn't.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:28.110
There's data that comes in.

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:30.980
You do something with the data,
and you put it somewhere else.

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:33.380
Oh MySQL, Oracle, Spanner--

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:34.280
doesn't matter.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:35.990
They all have their
own little protocol.

00:28:35.990 --> 00:28:37.810
They're going to store
your data in some format.

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:38.840
The bytes are the same.

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:41.670
I know you want to say is
cloud native, or new, or old.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:43.820
Legacy, it's all noise to me.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:45.002
There's the fundamentals.

00:28:45.002 --> 00:28:46.460
So when I look at
the fundamentals,

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:47.840
bytes are moving around.

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:49.370
How do you want to
move your bytes?

00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:51.130
You want to move it with Envoy?

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:54.980
Envoy has to move the
same bytes in the same way

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:56.160
that everything else has.

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:58.190
It's free to have
a new interface.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:00.357
It's free to do different
performance things.

00:29:00.357 --> 00:29:01.940
But at the end of
the day, those bytes

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:04.240
have to flow in a certain
set of directions.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:04.740
That's it.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:07.198
So when I see new technology
that comes out for networking,

00:29:07.198 --> 00:29:08.280
it's very simple.

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:10.720
These bytes need to go
from point A to point B.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:12.470
What are you going to
do with those bytes?

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:14.240
And the same thing
is true for compute.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:18.530
You're going to call, instruct
in sets on this kernel,

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:19.380
on this CPU.

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:20.630
You want to do it with Golang?

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:21.890
You want to do it with Python?

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.240
You want to do it with Docker?

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:24.657
At the end of the
day, you're just

00:29:24.657 --> 00:29:26.510
going to make some system calls.

00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:28.727
I never get tripped up and
confused with the noise.

00:29:28.727 --> 00:29:30.310
I just want to know,
how are you going

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:31.770
to do the fundamental thing?

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:34.190
And that's how I find bugs,
and that's how I work through.

00:29:34.190 --> 00:29:37.490
That's all it ever can
be is the fundamentals.

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:40.160
And then if you respect that,
and you learn the fundamentals,

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:41.630
any new technology
that comes out,

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:43.210
you only have to
ask one question.

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:45.140
How are you implementing
the fundamentals?

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:48.567
And then that's my
secret to doing that.

00:29:48.567 --> 00:29:51.150
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: There's another
question that-- so I'm going--

00:29:51.150 --> 00:29:53.790
I've been pulling these
out of LinkedIn and Twitter

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:55.950
that came for Matt.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:30:00.700
You developed a wonderful
open, like a data plane proxy.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:04.625
Why did you not build out the
control plane in open source?

00:30:04.625 --> 00:30:06.125
MATT KLEIN: That's
a great question,

00:30:06.125 --> 00:30:07.542
and it actually
comes back to what

00:30:07.542 --> 00:30:09.060
we were talking about before.

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:13.230
It's Envoy is a very
general technology.

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:15.220
And it's extensible.

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:16.260
It has a bunch of APIs.

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:17.760
Like I was saying
before, we've seen

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:20.410
it adopted in a tremendous
amount of use cases.

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:23.180
And like Anna was saying, if
it didn't have all the knobs,

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:25.140
if it wasn't that base
piece of technology,

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:28.230
it would not have been
adopted in all these cases.

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:31.170
What you find at
Lyft is that we have

00:30:31.170 --> 00:30:33.990
a control plane based on Envoy.

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.480
We built Envoy, so
the control plane

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:40.590
has built up over the
last three or four years.

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:42.480
The control plane's a
little embarrassing.

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:47.100
I would not open source it.

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:53.070
It's a mishmash of, like, Python
templating, and Bash, and Go.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:57.100
I mean, you all understand
how these things go.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:02.610
And my point is that we don't
expose all of the Envoy knobs

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:03.750
to people at Lyft.

00:31:03.750 --> 00:31:05.340
We have service manifests.

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:07.080
We have a configuration
generator.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.570
We do all these things,
so that people at Lyft

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:13.110
only see a very tiny subset
of the Envoy configuration.

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.050
But that also means that
the control plane at Lyft

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:19.390
is very specific to Lyft.

00:31:19.390 --> 00:31:21.030
I work for Lyft.

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:23.250
I'm not building a product.

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:25.800
And it just doesn't
make sense to invest

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:28.890
in trying to clean up that
control plane in a way

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:29.995
to open source it.

00:31:29.995 --> 00:31:32.370
That doesn't mean that we
haven't open sourced libraries.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:35.430
There are libraries that we
support in Envoy open source,

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:37.500
like Go control plane
and Java control plane.

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:39.690
These are foundational
libraries.

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:41.220
But they're not
complete solutions,

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:44.900
because they depend on our
Jenkins, and our Kubernetes,

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:47.520
and our Legacy VM
infrastructure.

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:50.640
Because we're not, of course,
not only on Kubernetes.

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:52.740
So it's really, again,
just about those

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:53.988
layered abstractions.

00:31:53.988 --> 00:31:55.530
And it just doesn't
make sense for us

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:58.317
to invest in open sourcing
that control plane.

00:31:58.317 --> 00:32:00.150
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
there's another question.

00:32:00.150 --> 00:32:01.043
Thank you, Matt.

00:32:01.043 --> 00:32:02.460
And there's a
question for Bikash.

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:05.310
So this person is
a Twitter user.

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:08.850
The person says that you
built these massive systems

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:11.490
at Google, and they've
been to all of your talks.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:16.590
They're like, now, as you are
at Juniper, which learnings

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:17.820
are you utilizing at Juniper?

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:19.278
And what are you
doing differently?

00:32:19.278 --> 00:32:21.570
BIKASH KOLEY: Yeah,
I think the best way

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:28.260
that I can describe this is,
building systems in Google

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:31.650
has certain expectations
with who your customers are.

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:36.060
And those customers inside of
Google, they're very savvy.

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:37.680
They're very tech savvy.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:39.570
So you want to
expose all the knobs.

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:41.347
You want to make it
highly programmable.

00:32:41.347 --> 00:32:43.680
You expect that there is a
[? suite ?] on the other side

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:45.570
of the system, who
is doing something

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:48.510
very meaningful with the
system that you've built.

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:51.150
In many of the
customers, the level

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:53.760
of programmability that they
can consume is different.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:55.150
And it's a spectrum.

00:32:55.150 --> 00:32:56.850
It's not the same for everybody.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:59.140
For many, usability is
extremely important.

00:32:59.140 --> 00:33:02.880
And so my mission
in life really has

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:05.043
been still build open system.

00:33:05.043 --> 00:33:06.960
Still build system that
is truly programmable.

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:09.630
Build system that is open
API, open data model.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:12.300
But also, build
usability on top,

00:33:12.300 --> 00:33:15.690
where, if somebody wants to
consume a truly modern system,

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:19.710
they can without having to
really build the development

00:33:19.710 --> 00:33:22.520
team that Google
has or can afford.

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:25.340
And that has been a learning--

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:28.070
sort of talking to, really,
spending a lot of time

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:32.220
with customers and seeing
they all have the same need.

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:33.960
I think Anna was
making the point

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:35.220
that Google is not a bubble.

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:38.740
Ultimately, everybody has need
for similar infrastructure,

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:40.470
whether they consume
it from cloud

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:42.830
or whether they
consume it internally.

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:45.060
But making it usable
for them and making

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:48.318
it usable without having
to make it proprietary,

00:33:48.318 --> 00:33:49.860
there is a bit of
a tension in there.

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:53.883
But I think it's doable,
and that has been my goal.

00:33:53.883 --> 00:33:56.050
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: And then
the last question for Anna,

00:33:56.050 --> 00:33:59.780
and then we'll open up to one or
two questions for the audience.

00:33:59.780 --> 00:34:02.370
Like today, people saw the
Traffic Director announcement.

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:04.620
They know you're
heavily involved.

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:07.140
As you ship out all of these
Google-specific products,

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:10.830
should they use it, or should
they be worried about lock-in?

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:14.219
ANNA BERENBERG: Traffic
Director has open interface

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:18.659
to Envoy's or any
XTS-compliant proxy.

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:23.320
So it's the way to merge
together all the value

00:34:23.320 --> 00:34:28.590
proposition as Google
developed over these 15 years

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:32.610
with the open interfaces
and essentially

00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:34.179
producing open systems.

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:40.170
So let's say you use
Traffic Director today.

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:42.370
And tomorrow, you decided
you don't want to use it.

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:43.880
You build your
own control plane.

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:50.130
There's nothing that locks
you in into Traffic Director,

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:52.710
because you just switch
your point at which you're

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:55.230
sending these APIs.

00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:59.610
And you get a different
control plane functionality

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:01.110
over the same APIs.

00:35:01.110 --> 00:35:02.880
We're hopeful that
you're not going

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:05.610
to switch away, because
we are providing

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:07.710
amazing functionality.

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:10.410
But I'm not going
to be selling here.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:13.710
But in general, as long as
you have open interfaces,

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:15.970
there is no vendor lock-in.

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:20.730
So in my mind, the
data plane needs

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:25.440
to be universally open
source, like Envoy or JRPC,

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:27.780
that is without a proxy.

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:31.020
This basic transport
needs to be open source.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:34.020
And then you have control planes
that are sitting on top of it,

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:39.030
with open APIs, which are
also removing vendors lock-in.

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:42.870
And then the cloud providers
can compete on the value

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:46.370
proposition behind these APIs.

00:35:46.370 --> 00:35:48.530
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So I
did want the audience--

00:35:48.530 --> 00:35:51.290
I want to give them the chance
to ask one or two questions.

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:53.040
And then you can find
them after the talk,

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:55.850
because I do want to end
with their perspective.

00:35:55.850 --> 00:35:57.630
If you want to ask a
question, we probably

00:35:57.630 --> 00:35:58.880
have only time for one or two.

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:01.845
But just use one of the
mics so everybody can hear.

00:36:01.845 --> 00:36:03.220
AUDIENCE: Just a
question for you

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:06.190
with regards to
Mongo's experiment

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:09.532
with the server-side license.

00:36:09.532 --> 00:36:11.240
What do you think
Mongo should have done?

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:16.076
And what do you feel about the
community's reaction to it?

00:36:16.076 --> 00:36:19.507
No volunteers?

00:36:19.507 --> 00:36:21.340
MATT KLEIN: Well, you
know, this is actually

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:23.590
what I was talking about before.

00:36:23.590 --> 00:36:30.950
Look, no one does anything in
this industry out of goodwill,

00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:31.450
right?

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:32.658
I mean, let's just be honest.

00:36:32.658 --> 00:36:34.360
We're all here
probably to make--

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:35.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:35.140 --> 00:36:38.520
We're all here to make
money in some way.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:40.600
Some of us are selling
infrastructure.

00:36:40.600 --> 00:36:42.520
Some of us are
building businesses.

00:36:42.520 --> 00:36:45.820
But very few of us, if we're
really honest with ourselves,

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:49.240
are here because this is the
best thing that we can be

00:36:49.240 --> 00:36:51.940
doing on Wednesday afternoon.

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:57.020
So with that said, if you look
around at people's motivations,

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:01.330
they're trying to build a
business out of their software.

00:37:01.330 --> 00:37:03.850
The problem right now--
and I don't really

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:06.670
want to comment
on Mongo or Redis,

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:11.050
just because everyone has
their own business situation

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:12.500
that they're in.

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:14.080
But this is directly
related to what

00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:16.180
I was saying before
about why I didn't start

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:20.770
my own company, which is, there
is a tension between locking

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:25.030
down open systems and software
and trying to charge people

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:28.960
for those systems versus
building a welcoming, larger

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:32.470
community, which is extensible,
and then allows everyone

00:37:32.470 --> 00:37:33.940
to succeed.

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:35.650
And this is very subtle.

00:37:35.650 --> 00:37:37.480
I've written a blog
post on this, which you

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.310
could check out, if you want.

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:42.010
But I think there
are ways of building

00:37:42.010 --> 00:37:44.320
viable vertical
businesses on top

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:48.520
without alienating
people on locking down

00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:51.380
certain pieces of the software.

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:54.790
So what I would say, again,
without specifically commenting

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:57.430
on particular
companies, I think there

00:37:57.430 --> 00:37:59.650
are ways that it can
be done, but it's

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:02.223
a very complicated problem.

00:38:02.223 --> 00:38:03.640
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
So we can probably

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:04.450
take one more question.

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:04.930
Oh, you wanted to add?

00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:05.430
Go ahead.

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:07.780
BIKASH KOLEY:
Maybe I'll add, not

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:11.080
answering specifically
to MongoDB, again,

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:15.250
because I don't know what their
business model specifically is.

00:38:15.250 --> 00:38:17.900
I think there is a misconception
that open source is free.

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:20.200
It's not free.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:22.900
The software developers
have to make a living

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:25.000
by writing software
somehow, and there

00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:27.190
has to be a business model
somehow for the software

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:31.560
developers to make
money, to make a living.

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:34.300
The part that I found
is very important

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:38.290
is to be very clear
about the business model

00:38:38.290 --> 00:38:41.680
that you have around open
source, which sometimes is not

00:38:41.680 --> 00:38:42.520
true.

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:45.670
And changing the business
model mid-course actually

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:48.020
can be very problematic
for the community.

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:49.610
And that's the problem.

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:52.960
The problem is, if we don't
have the business model laid out

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:55.583
very clearly from day
one, Tungsten Fabric

00:38:55.583 --> 00:38:57.250
has a business model
of open core, where

00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:58.958
the stuff that is open
source will always

00:38:58.958 --> 00:39:00.130
remain open source, period.

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:02.168
It doesn't matter whether
a Juniper competition

00:39:02.168 --> 00:39:04.210
is building a product with
us at Tungsten Fabric.

00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:05.585
It's going to
remain open source,

00:39:05.585 --> 00:39:08.837
because that's the charter
in Linux Foundation.

00:39:08.837 --> 00:39:11.170
And I think, having that
clarity with the business model

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:12.863
is important, because
otherwise, people

00:39:12.863 --> 00:39:15.280
build this mistaken assumption
that somehow open source is

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:16.220
free.

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:19.270
And now, you try to figure
out how to monetize it.

00:39:19.270 --> 00:39:20.800
And that's the problem.

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:23.300
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Kelsey says,
let's take some more questions.

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:25.240
So let's take some
more questions.

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:26.940
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: We can be
really tight with our answers

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:27.550
as well.

00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:29.185
I mean, you guys
are here in person.

00:39:29.185 --> 00:39:30.060
So we appreciate that

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:31.660
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: And it's
your panel, so go for it.

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:33.952
If you want to ask questions,
just use one of the mics,

00:39:33.952 --> 00:39:36.783
though, so everybody can hear.

00:39:36.783 --> 00:39:38.200
AUDIENCE: The way
I see Kubernetes

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:40.690
is that it's a level playing
field for the way you

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:43.240
build and deploy
containerized applications.

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:45.400
And Kubernetes is
available both on-prem

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:46.650
and all the public clouds.

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:52.780
Do we need an answer for
network security storage

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:56.036
that's similar to Kubernetes?

00:39:56.036 --> 00:39:57.410
And do we have an answer?

00:39:59.895 --> 00:40:01.270
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
The thing is, I

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:03.220
think, you're going
to need someone

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:06.510
with that level of
experience willing to build

00:40:06.510 --> 00:40:09.070
a system in a very similar way
that Envoy did and Kubernetes

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:11.840
did, and, I think, that's
going to be very hard.

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:15.020
We saw it with Git, with
Sourcetree management.

00:40:15.020 --> 00:40:16.270
We saw it with Linux.

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:18.520
So I think a lot of people
have the right motivations,

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:19.978
but you need all
the other factors.

00:40:19.978 --> 00:40:22.480
And I think it's very rare
that you'll see an Envoy come

00:40:22.480 --> 00:40:24.250
from a place that it came from.

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:27.400
I think it needs to solve a
real problem first for at least

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:30.127
one company first
and put it out there.

00:40:30.127 --> 00:40:31.960
And if people think it
can be generic enough

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:33.580
that a lot of people
want to use it,

00:40:33.580 --> 00:40:34.930
I think that'll work out well.

00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:37.232
We saw this with HTTP.

00:40:37.232 --> 00:40:38.440
There's a standard around it.

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:39.817
Lots of people can implement it.

00:40:39.817 --> 00:40:42.400
But the problem with networking,
it's a lot of cultural things

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.627
that are baked into how
you lay out your network.

00:40:45.627 --> 00:40:46.960
There's different architectures.

00:40:46.960 --> 00:40:49.600
To me, networking is closer
to programming languages.

00:40:49.600 --> 00:40:52.600
You can decide what architecture
you want your network to take.

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:55.600
Some infosec teams won't give
out more than 10 IP addresses.

00:40:55.600 --> 00:40:58.930
They count them, like, no,
no, no, these are private IPs,

00:40:58.930 --> 00:40:59.948
I can give you 10.

00:40:59.948 --> 00:41:01.990
It's like, what the hell
are you doing with them?

00:41:01.990 --> 00:41:03.130
They're fake, actually.

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:05.050
You can make more of them.

00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:07.153
It's very cultural.

00:41:07.153 --> 00:41:08.695
To me, I think the
networking problem

00:41:08.695 --> 00:41:11.480
is that it's so cultural
for these companies,

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:13.360
if I gave you the
best networking

00:41:13.360 --> 00:41:15.790
tool, like in Kubernetes,
the culture is going

00:41:15.790 --> 00:41:17.380
to dictate how it gets used.

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:19.060
That's the features
you're going to ask.

00:41:19.060 --> 00:41:20.410
So I think Kubernetes
is a little bit easier,

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:21.250
because again,
you're just making

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:23.740
some system calls, wrap up your
code, put it in a container.

00:41:23.740 --> 00:41:24.580
It's going to run it for you.

00:41:24.580 --> 00:41:25.600
So I don't know.

00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:26.850
Some of them may try to do it.

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:28.240
I think Envoy gets
us very close.

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:30.550
But in terms of how you lay
out architected framework

00:41:30.550 --> 00:41:33.120
for your internal network,
I don't know about that one.

00:41:36.450 --> 00:41:38.160
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Any
other questions?

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:39.420
We can probably take one more.

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:43.300
Otherwise, I want to put the
panelists on the hot spots.

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:44.550
So I can do that instead.

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:45.210
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Do it.

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:46.127
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Do it?

00:41:46.127 --> 00:41:46.650
OK.

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:50.618
So each of you, I think
you rightly pointed out

00:41:50.618 --> 00:41:53.160
that different companies have
different motivations for doing

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:54.720
all of this.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:58.200
When I look at each one of
you, there are several things,

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:00.630
as Kelsey pointed out, we
have a very heavy-duty title

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:01.663
for this talk.

00:42:01.663 --> 00:42:03.330
And so I wanted to
put each of the folks

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:06.450
here on the hot seat to
answer a few questions.

00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:08.280
We spoke so much
about open systems

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:11.730
and making the switch from
whatever your deployment is

00:42:11.730 --> 00:42:13.590
today, and then
somehow migrating it

00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:17.220
to whatever your end
goal is, so bringing

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:19.630
all of this to your brownfield
and so on and so forth.

00:42:19.630 --> 00:42:23.625
But if you, let's say, look
two to three years ahead,

00:42:23.625 --> 00:42:25.500
let's say, we have all
of these open systems.

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:27.250
We have all of these paradigms.

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:28.302
What has it enabled?

00:42:28.302 --> 00:42:29.760
What kind of
businesses do you see?

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:31.040
What kind of problems solved?

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:32.610
What kind of communities?

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:35.730
Are we even going to be talking
about Kubernetes, and Envoy,

00:42:35.730 --> 00:42:37.420
and Server Smash,
and all of that two,

00:42:37.420 --> 00:42:38.503
three years down the line?

00:42:38.503 --> 00:42:41.010
Or will the conversation
be totally different?

00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:44.350
And maybe we can start
with you, Bikash.

00:42:44.350 --> 00:42:49.560
BIKASH KOLEY: So I think the
very nature of applications

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:52.590
and users are evolving, so it's
a given that the infrastructure

00:42:52.590 --> 00:42:54.150
is going to evolve.

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:56.880
I think that's one thing
that we know for sure.

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:59.620
Everything else is
pretty much a guess.

00:42:59.620 --> 00:43:05.520
I do think that being open
is a necessity in the world

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:06.330
that we live in.

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:10.290
And the reason being,
ultimately, now, sometimes

00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:12.480
I get this question
of, what is cloud?

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:14.400
It's a set of stable
APIs on an infrastructure

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:16.210
that the APIs hide from you.

00:43:16.210 --> 00:43:17.075
That's what it is.

00:43:17.075 --> 00:43:18.450
And of course,
the infrastructure

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:19.420
evolves over time.

00:43:19.420 --> 00:43:20.962
It's true for
everybody, whether it's

00:43:20.962 --> 00:43:23.700
public cloud or private cloud.

00:43:23.700 --> 00:43:25.800
It's the well-defined
abstractions in API

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:28.140
that make it durable
and usable, right?

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:33.600
So I think the need for
open is coming from the fact

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:35.700
that you want a part
of the infrastructure

00:43:35.700 --> 00:43:37.492
that you can use for
a long period of time,

00:43:37.492 --> 00:43:39.510
while the underlying
scaffoldings will

00:43:39.510 --> 00:43:40.690
change over time.

00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:42.870
And you don't have to
reinvent your infrastructure

00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:43.695
every single time.

00:43:43.695 --> 00:43:44.820
So I think we've seen that.

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:49.920
If you look back 20 years
in how [INAUDIBLE] was done,

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:53.568
the basic Unix system calls
haven't changed that much.

00:43:53.568 --> 00:43:55.860
[? Downlink ?] scaffoldings
have significantly changed.

00:43:58.750 --> 00:44:01.600
MATT KLEIN: I think
my view and the thing

00:44:01.600 --> 00:44:06.250
that I typically tell people is
that all of us that are sitting

00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:09.250
here building
infrastructure, we're

00:44:09.250 --> 00:44:12.310
doing it to support application
developers building business

00:44:12.310 --> 00:44:12.820
logic.

00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:13.900
That's the entire point.

00:44:13.900 --> 00:44:15.730
I mean, infrastructure
is overhead.

00:44:15.730 --> 00:44:17.110
There's no point to it, right?

00:44:17.110 --> 00:44:20.920
The way that people make money
is by writing business logic.

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:24.220
So I think my goal-- and
this is not a three year out.

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:27.560
This is more of a
five to 10 year out.

00:44:27.560 --> 00:44:31.150
I think ultimately, I do
believe in the, quote,

00:44:31.150 --> 00:44:33.070
"serverless paradigms."

00:44:33.070 --> 00:44:36.550
And just by that, I mean that
in the long run, as Kelsey

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:40.240
was saying, you want people
to write code, and store data,

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:41.320
and talk to things.

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:41.900
That's it.

00:44:41.900 --> 00:44:43.990
That's all application
developers care about that.

00:44:43.990 --> 00:44:45.670
They don't care about
container systems.

00:44:45.670 --> 00:44:46.880
They don't care
about networking.

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:48.130
They don't care about routing.

00:44:48.130 --> 00:44:49.780
They don't care about failover.

00:44:49.780 --> 00:44:53.620
And so I guess, my
view is that our goal

00:44:53.620 --> 00:44:56.080
is to make it so that no
one even cares or knows

00:44:56.080 --> 00:44:57.280
about Kubernetes.

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:59.800
They don't care or
know about Envoy.

00:44:59.800 --> 00:45:03.130
Envoy is a transparent thing
that provides functionality,

00:45:03.130 --> 00:45:05.600
and all people are doing
is the fundamentals.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:08.320
And we're so far away
from that right now.

00:45:08.320 --> 00:45:10.510
And that's what
excites me is that we

00:45:10.510 --> 00:45:14.470
have many years of building
better abstractions and better

00:45:14.470 --> 00:45:16.440
systems so that
the fundamentals,

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:20.592
they just get to people
in a much simpler fashion.

00:45:20.592 --> 00:45:22.050
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Yeah, so I think,

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:24.137
if you look at
programming languages,

00:45:24.137 --> 00:45:26.220
like if you look at neural
programming languages--

00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:28.840
I would even put Python in
that category and Golang--

00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:32.340
they have very extensive
standard libraries.

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:35.130
Go has this very
robust HTP server,

00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:37.575
where typically, you don't
need to front it with anything.

00:45:37.575 --> 00:45:40.200
And a lot of the libraries that
you see in the standard library

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:42.480
allow you to compose
just software that's

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:43.770
ready to go on the network.

00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:45.030
That was Go's goal.

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:47.280
If you think about this from
the platform perspective,

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:49.710
so platforms are evolving from
these bare-metal machines,

00:45:49.710 --> 00:45:52.260
Linux systemd init systems.

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:53.760
And now that were
in this Kubernetes

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:57.330
era, serverless eras, I think
Kubernetes and Envoy just

00:45:57.330 --> 00:45:59.352
become standard
libraries for platforms.

00:45:59.352 --> 00:46:00.810
If you're going to
build a platform

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:02.185
to talk about
Matt's goal, you're

00:46:02.185 --> 00:46:04.268
just going to look at these
as standard libraries.

00:46:04.268 --> 00:46:06.930
All right, I need somebody
to handle this network layer.

00:46:06.930 --> 00:46:08.940
I need something to
handle the compute layer.

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:11.530
You just going to import
those into your stack.

00:46:11.530 --> 00:46:13.410
And the thing that
you build on top

00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:14.760
will be that higher level thing.

00:46:14.760 --> 00:46:16.410
That's what Go's
trying to do for you.

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:17.880
You build programming logic.

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:19.980
You don't think
about SSL handshakes.

00:46:19.980 --> 00:46:21.630
You don't think
about HTP handshakes.

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:23.730
All you do is
import that library.

00:46:23.730 --> 00:46:26.430
You write your handler,
and you handle the request.

00:46:26.430 --> 00:46:27.920
These things that
we're working on

00:46:27.920 --> 00:46:30.355
is all so you can have
that platform at top.

00:46:30.355 --> 00:46:31.980
So I think that's
the best way to start

00:46:31.980 --> 00:46:33.730
thinking about the
future of these things.

00:46:33.730 --> 00:46:35.850
Start at, what do
you want to do?

00:46:35.850 --> 00:46:37.392
What's the minimum
required to do it?

00:46:37.392 --> 00:46:38.850
And all the things
that are getting

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:40.860
in your way of the minimal
requirement, that's

00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:43.510
the things that we probably need
to fit, flesh out, and build.

00:46:43.510 --> 00:46:45.135
But that's the thing
that will underpin

00:46:45.135 --> 00:46:48.170
the long-term platform.

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:50.910
ANNA BERENBERG: I agree with
everything being said here.

00:46:50.910 --> 00:46:56.880
But I always say that in five
years, network will disappear.

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.370
Nobody wants to program
networking, right?

00:46:59.370 --> 00:47:03.100
Find one developer who
actually interested in it.

00:47:03.100 --> 00:47:06.900
So networking will
disappear, all

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:08.690
that contain management also.

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:11.070
And over here, you'll
have a different panel.

00:47:11.070 --> 00:47:14.850
You'll have a panel of
business developers, who

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:17.820
are going to be talking to
each other how to federate,

00:47:17.820 --> 00:47:20.200
how to make their
vertical into horizontals.

00:47:20.200 --> 00:47:22.590
Because at that time,
the conversation

00:47:22.590 --> 00:47:25.600
will move a layer up.

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:28.500
And I'm not saying that we're
going to become dinosaurs.

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:32.850
But by that time, we
either solve the problem,

00:47:32.850 --> 00:47:35.080
or we're going to be
where we are today.

00:47:35.080 --> 00:47:40.500
So we need to solve the problem
to become universal, very much

00:47:40.500 --> 00:47:44.820
horizontal across
different environment,

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:50.370
make workloads be the same,
no matter where they run.

00:47:50.370 --> 00:47:54.490
And basically, solve the
problem that we discussed today.

00:47:54.490 --> 00:47:56.240
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I
want to add one thing.

00:47:56.240 --> 00:47:57.630
The time horizon,
everyone thinks

00:47:57.630 --> 00:47:59.340
that this is five years away.

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:02.100
I was at KubeCon, and
everyone's talking

00:48:02.100 --> 00:48:04.565
about Kubernetes and Service
Meshes, and all these things.

00:48:04.565 --> 00:48:06.940
And there was a person, there
was a Google Cloud T-shirt.

00:48:06.940 --> 00:48:08.070
They were a customer.

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:10.170
And they were like, I
am so glad we just used

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:11.550
App Engine eight years ago.

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:13.650
I don't care about any of that.

00:48:13.650 --> 00:48:15.300
And they run a real business.

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:16.890
It's not a fancy startup.

00:48:16.890 --> 00:48:17.867
They just don't care.

00:48:17.867 --> 00:48:19.950
And they're like, why are
people trying to rebuild

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:21.970
App Engine from scratch?

00:48:21.970 --> 00:48:24.173
And so the thing is, some
people have this reality.

00:48:24.173 --> 00:48:26.590
I'm not saying that it's going
to be reality for everyone.

00:48:26.590 --> 00:48:29.723
There are people who've had
this reality for a long time.

00:48:29.723 --> 00:48:31.890
I think most people, they
just convinced themselves,

00:48:31.890 --> 00:48:34.800
you're just trying to
build the same thing.

00:48:34.800 --> 00:48:37.050
You just feel more comfortable
doing it from, I think,

00:48:37.050 --> 00:48:37.720
the steps.

00:48:37.720 --> 00:48:39.050
This is your stepping stones.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:40.223
But if you step back--

00:48:40.223 --> 00:48:41.640
and I tell a lot
of our customers,

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:43.682
you really want to build
App Engine from scratch?

00:48:43.682 --> 00:48:44.610
I'll show you how.

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.235
But just know that's
what you're doing.

00:48:49.100 --> 00:48:52.580
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yes, I think the
reason for getting all of these

00:48:52.580 --> 00:48:54.890
folks here-- and we're almost
at the end of the panel,

00:48:54.890 --> 00:48:56.870
because they wouldn't
give us more time--

00:48:56.870 --> 00:48:59.210
but the reason for
bringing all of these folks

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:01.698
into these conversations
are starting--

00:49:01.698 --> 00:49:03.990
these are just the beginnings
of a lot of conversations

00:49:03.990 --> 00:49:06.230
that are on open
systems, open interfaces.

00:49:06.230 --> 00:49:08.420
As each one of us here
goes and builds out

00:49:08.420 --> 00:49:10.940
these technologies
in open source,

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:14.220
even within Google, at Juniper,
and so on and so forth,

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:16.220
and the whole goal
was to get you folks

00:49:16.220 --> 00:49:18.920
as well thinking about
the open interfaces.

00:49:18.920 --> 00:49:21.080
Also, how whatever
it is that you build

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:24.200
has a value for the community to
also benefit from that and also

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:25.430
your own business as well.

00:49:25.430 --> 00:49:29.330
Because everybody is in it to
create a business out of things

00:49:29.330 --> 00:49:31.230
or to solve a
particular problem.

00:49:31.230 --> 00:49:33.170
And so one of the things
I highly encourage

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:35.630
is to also engage with
these folks on Twitter,

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:39.200
LinkedIn, other forums, and then
keep the conversations going.

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:41.700
So thank you for coming, and
thanks to all of our panelists.

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:42.867
Please give them a big hand.

00:49:42.867 --> 00:49:43.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:46.570
